WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.790
ANTHONY BUSHONG: So Steph,
why are we in your garage?

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:03.270
STEPHANIE WONG: I
thought you said

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.584
we were building stuff, right?

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:06.000
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
No, I was talking

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.090
about automating builds with
Google Cloud Build, not that.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Oh,
Google Cloud Build.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.690
You mean the one that
lets you orchestrate

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.419
build steps that run
as container images.

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:15.460
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Exactly.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.918
And this means that
each build step

00:00:16.918 --> 00:00:19.080
can do anything a container
can, such as executing

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.267
a script or a binary.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:22.350
So let's look at how you
can use Cloud Build today

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.352
to automate a simple
Terraform workflow in a way

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:26.810
that you might see a small team
run infrastructure as code.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.010
Now, as a developer, I may want
to edit our team's Terraform

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:42.000
code on a dev branch to create
a new node pool to accommodate

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.840
a more CPU-intensive workload
than our Kubernetes Engine

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.150
cluster currently allows for.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.000
I can do this by updating
our Terraform code

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.770
with a new node pool resource.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.280
This node pool will be
called Olympic Pool.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.220
And we can commit those changes
to a development branch.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.500
This way we can
actually use Cloud Build

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.500
to create an approval workflow
as opposed to me actually

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.410
applying changes to our
infrastructure from Terraform

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.700
locally.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.650
We'll do this using
Cloud Build's notion

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:11.917
of build triggers.

00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:13.500
You'll see that my
first build trigger

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.840
is set to kick off a
build off of any push

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.050
to a non-master branch.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.300
Because I changed the
Terraform configuration

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.950
on the dev branch, our build
has already been triggered.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.770
So when we actually
dive into the build,

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.950
you'll see that it's comprised
of two distinct build steps,

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.620
each running in a container
with the Terraform binary.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.700
The first build step will
initialize Terraform,

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.780
and the second will
show us what resources

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.060
will be created with
the updated code

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.330
without actually
creating those resources.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:47.210
In this case, it will
be one new node pool.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.640
And we can actually see
this in the logs from those

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.832
build steps in the
Cloud Build UI.

00:01:52.832 --> 00:01:55.040
STEPHANIE WONG: So if we
wanted to, we could run more

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.800
build steps to, let's
say, run test suites,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.801
or actually apply this
to a staging environment.

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:00.925
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Precisely.

00:02:00.925 --> 00:02:01.880
That's the idea.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.470
This is a very
simple illustration.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.540
Build steps are actually
customizable to the point where

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.623
you should use them to
represent the workflow that

00:02:07.623 --> 00:02:08.375
fits your culture.

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:10.250
So the two use cases
that you just mentioned,

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.020
they're definitely patterns
that you can adopt.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.311
STEPHANIE WONG: So in this
demo, how will these changes

00:02:14.311 --> 00:02:15.530
be realized in production?

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.821
ANTHONY BUSHONG: With another
build trigger, of course.

00:02:17.821 --> 00:02:20.270
So once I've actually pushed
those changes to the dev

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.570
branch, we're actually going
to create a pull request

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.790
against the master branch.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.400
This is what will actually
allow for our infrastructure

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.554
to be realized in production.

00:02:30.554 --> 00:02:32.720
You'll see here that a new
node pool resource is now

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.870
going to be created.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.540
So we'll create
that pull request.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.654
Typically, this introduces
a level of approval

00:02:39.654 --> 00:02:41.070
where we can
actually have someone

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.110
with the proper permissions
review these changes

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.110
and give the approval to
actually merge the pull

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.730
request.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.720
So once we actually merge the
pull request into the master

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.480
branch, we will
actually be invoking

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.460
our second build trigger.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.430
This second build
trigger is actually

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.200
setup to run on any change
to the master branch

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.280
as defined here.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.780
And you'll see that we actually
have a different Cloud Build

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.020
YAML file for this
build trigger.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.880
In this case, what
we're doing here

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.830
is we're actually
adding a third build

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.320
step using the same
Terraform container

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.120
image that we had previously.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.580
In this case, we'll
now be able to apply

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.720
those actual infrastructure
changes with Terraform.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.750
So because we've actually
merged dev into master,

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.270
our build has been
triggered to actually run

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.160
with those three build steps.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.830
So you'll see here that we still
have the Terraform container

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.670
image running in it, plan, and
now we have the third step that

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.340
says apply, and auto approve.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.410
So it happens that's
because we actually

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.930
were able to merge
something into master,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.530
this build was then triggered
to actually apply those changes,

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.210
as opposed to the
previous build,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.670
which just ran a plan so
that we could introspect

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.076
what we wanted to create.

00:03:56.076 --> 00:03:57.700
So because that build
has actually run,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.720
let's look in our GKE cluster.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.590
And we'll see here that the
node pool called Olympic Pool is

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.060
actually being created.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.730
Google Cloud Build can easily
make your workflow automated

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.680
and help you easily enable
continuous building, testing,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.720
and deployment.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.636
STEPHANIE WONG: Don't
forget to like, comment,

00:04:14.636 --> 00:04:17.930
and subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

